::: {.doc-subject}

Calculate the Kirchhoff index (total resistance) of the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L85271-L85382){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.kirchhoff_index()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Kirchhoff index](../assets/graphs/kirchhoff-index.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A six-node graph labeled with the resistance distance across each edge: 1 on the two tail edges, but 0.75 on the four cycle edges (accent), where a second route runs in parallel. Summing resistance over all 15 pairs gives the Kirchhoff index 21.0." width="100%"}

The Kirchhoff index is the sum of all pairwise resistance distances:

`Kf(G) = Σᵢ<ⱼ Ω(i,j)`

It can also be computed from the Laplacian eigenvalues:

`Kf(G) = n × Σₖ (1/λₖ)`

where `λₖ` are the non-zero eigenvalues of the Laplacian matrix. This method uses the
eigenvalue form, which needs a single eigendecomposition.

The Kirchhoff index is a single-number summary of how well-connected a whole network is,
treating every edge as a 1-ohm resistor (see `resistance_distance()`). Lower values
mean the nodes are, on the whole, joined by more numerous and shorter routes. It is used
to:

- compare the overall robustness of different network designs with the same number of
  nodes
- characterize molecular graphs in chemistry (it was introduced as a molecular
  descriptor)
- measure how adding or removing an edge changes global connectivity

Edge weights are ignored, parallel edges act as resistors in parallel, and self-loops are
ignored. The value grows with the number of nodes, so compare graphs of similar size, or
divide by the number of node pairs for an average resistance. The computation is a dense
`O(n^3)` eigendecomposition, which becomes slow for graphs with many thousands of nodes.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The Kirchhoff index. Returns `0.0` for graphs with fewer than two nodes, and infinity
    (`inf`) for disconnected graphs.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is directed.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The Kirchhoff index $Kf(G) = \sum_{i < j} \Omega(i, j)$ (Klein and Randić, 1993) is
the electrical analogue of the Wiener index, using effective resistance in place of
shortest-path distance. A striking spectral identity ties it to the Laplacian
directly: $Kf(G) = n \sum_{k \ge 2} \tfrac{1}{\lambda_k}$, summing reciprocals of
the non-zero Laplacian eigenvalues, which makes small $\lambda_2$ (poor expansion)
inflate $Kf$ and sharp expander graphs bring it down. Complete graphs saturate the
lower bound $Kf \ge n - 1$; disconnected graphs give $+\infty$ because some
resistances diverge (detected as more than one zero eigenvalue). Cost is $O(n^3)$
via full Laplacian eigendecomposition, and only undirected inputs make sense
because the electrical analogue requires reversibility.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~wiener_index`) 

:   Sum path distances instead of resistances.

[](`~resistance_distance_matrix`) 

:   The resistances that the index adds up.

[](`~laplacian_spectrum`) 

:   The eigenvalues the index is computed from.

[](`~kemeny_constant`) 

:   A related measure of random walks.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Closing a path of four nodes into a cycle gives every pair a second route, and halves
the index:

::: {#bf1801e1 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

path = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D")])
cycle = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "A")]
)

round(path.kirchhoff_index(), 3), round(cycle.kirchhoff_index(), 3)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(10.0, 5.0)
```
:::
:::


### Rings and chains

`molecules()` holds the graphs of seven small molecules, one node per atom (leaving out
the hydrogens) and one edge per bond. In a molecule without rings there is one route
between any two atoms, so resistance and distance are the same, and the Kirchhoff index
equals the Wiener index. In a ring, each pair has two routes and the index is lower:

::: {#bfe958b4 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl

molecules = ec.molecules()

pl.DataFrame(
    {
        "molecule": list(molecules),
        "wiener": [g.wiener_index() for g in molecules.values()],
        "kirchhoff": [
            round(g.kirchhoff_index(), 3) for g in molecules.values()
        ],
    }
)
```

